    { name: 'Melanocortin 1 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_1_receptor'] },
    { name: 'Dihydroxyacetone', type: 'ChemicalSubstance', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dihydroxyacetone'] },
    { name: 'Forskolin', type: 'ChemicalSubstance', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forskolin'] },
  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      '“Topical tanning” covers two completely different things: fake tans that stain the dead surface of your skin, and experimental agents that switch on your own melanin from the outside.',
      'Today’s self-tan lotions work by DHA, a sugar that browns dead surface proteins in a Maillard (browning) reaction — a temporary surface dye, not melanin, with no meaningful sun protection.',
      'Genuine topical tanning has been shown in the lab: forskolin darkened pale mice through the cAMP pathway, and engineered SIK inhibitors darkened human skin samples — but neither is an approved or marketed product.',
      'The hard part is the barrier: human epidermis blocks most molecules, which is why mouse results don’t automatically transfer and why a melanotan cream can’t work — peptides don’t cross skin.',
      'The prize is real eumelanin, the photoprotective pigment that shields DNA — so a true topical tan would be about skin-cancer protection as much as cosmetics. That remains a goal, not a product.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    "A tan from a bottle, no sun required" is one of the oldest promises in
    cosmetics — and it hides a genuine scientific split. There are two entirely
    different ways to make skin look darker without UV, and they share almost
    nothing. One <em>stains</em> the dead surface of your skin a browned colour.
    The other actually persuades your living pigment cells to build real melanin —
    the same thing the sun does, reached chemically instead. The first you can buy
    today; the second is one of the more interesting frontiers in pigment biology.
    This page separates them, and explains why the harder one has stayed in the lab.
    It is education, not medical advice or a product recommendation.
  </p>

  <h2>Two meanings of "a tan in a cream"</h2>
  <p>
    A real suntan is pigment cells (melanocytes) responding to a signal — UV light
    switches on <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>, which raises the messenger
    cyclic AMP and pushes the cell to make brown-black <em>eumelanin</em>, the
    pigment that shields DNA.<Cite n={3} /> Any "topical tan" is judged against that.
    The two routes to a darker look are:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A stain on dead skin</strong> — self-tan lotions, mousses and sprays.
      These colour the dead, outermost skin layer with a brown dye. No pigment cell
      is involved, nothing is signalled, and it fades as the surface sheds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A switch for real pigment</strong> — experimental creams that act on
      your own melanocytes, turning on genuine eumelanin from the outside. This is
      the one with real photoprotective potential, and the one that is still
      experimental.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    They look superficially similar in a mirror and could not be more different
    underneath. Confusing them is where most of the marketing — and most of the
    misplaced hope about "safe tanning" — comes from.
  </p>

  <h2>What today's fake tan actually does</h2>
  <p>
    The self-tanner in every pharmacy works through one ingredient:
    <strong>dihydroxyacetone (DHA)</strong>, a small sugar. When you rub it on, it
    reacts with amino acids in the proteins of your <em>stratum corneum</em> — the
    dead, flattened cells at the very surface — in a <em>Maillard reaction</em>, the
    same non-enzymatic browning chemistry that colours a seared steak or a slice of
    toast. The products are brown pigments called <em>melanoidins</em>.<Cite n={6} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Three consequences follow, and they define what a fake tan is and isn't:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>It is on dead cells, not in living ones.</strong> The colour lives in
      the surface layer that is already on its way off your body, so it fades over
      roughly three to seven days as those cells slough — no melanocyte ever
      participates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It is not melanin.</strong> Melanoidins are a different class of
      molecule, built by a chemical reaction rather than by a cell's pigment
      machinery, so none of melanin's biology comes with them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It does not protect you.</strong> A DHA tan offers at best a trivial,
      short-lived sun factor and nothing resembling eumelanin's DNA protection, and
      the reaction may even generate free radicals in skin, more so under UV.<Cite n={7} />
      The one real safety benefit of self-tanner is indirect: it can replace the
      urge to bake in UV in the first place. Normal sun protection still applies.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So a fake tan is a cosmetic surface stain — useful, mostly safe, and completely
    unrelated to the melanocortin pigment system this site is built around. The
    interesting science is the other route.
  </p>

  <h2>The real goal: switching on your own melanin, from the outside</h2>
  <p>
    The genuinely hard, genuinely valuable idea is a topical agent that reaches your
    melanocytes and tells them to make eumelanin without any UV. Why bother, when
    injected <a href="/melanotan">melanotan</a> already does something similar? Two
    reasons. A cream avoids a needle and an unregulated peptide of unknown quality.
    And more importantly, the pigment it would build is the <em>protective</em>
    kind — the landmark trials of the injected medical analogue showed melanocortin
    tanning cuts UV-induced DNA damage markers substantially — so a real topical tan
    is as much a skin-cancer-prevention idea as a cosmetic one.<Cite n={4} /> The
    biology is sound; the delivery is the wall.
  </p>

  <h3>Forskolin: the proof of concept in mice</h3>
  <p>
    The pivotal experiment came from David Fisher's group in 2006. Red-haired,
    fair-skinned mice carry a broken MC1R, so UV doesn't tan them — their pigment
    switch can't be flipped the normal way. The researchers reasoned that if you
    stimulate the pathway <em>downstream</em> of the faulty receptor, you should
    rescue the tan. They used <strong>forskolin</strong>, a plant compound that
    raises cyclic AMP directly, applied to the skin — and the pale mice turned dark,
    with no UV at all.<Cite n={1} /> Crucially, that induced pigment was
    <em>protective</em>: it reduced UV-driven DNA damage and skin tumours in a
    cancer-prone strain, and a 2009 follow-up showed prolonged topical forskolin
    produced a persistent, protective tan.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={5} /> This is the proof
    that a topical small molecule can build real, shielding melanin by borrowing the
    melanocortin pathway. It also proved the catch: it was done in mice, whose thin
    skin a drug crosses far more easily than ours.
  </p>

  <h3>SIK inhibitors: making it work on human skin</h3>
  <p>
    The 2006 result stalled on exactly that barrier — human epidermis is much less
    permeable, and forskolin didn't translate. The breakthrough came in 2017, again
    from the Fisher lab at Massachusetts General Hospital. Instead of forskolin they
    targeted <strong>SIK (salt-inducible kinase)</strong>, an enzyme that normally
    restrains <em>MITF</em>, the master transcription factor for pigment genes.
    Inhibit SIK and MITF is released, so the cell ramps up melanin — the same
    endpoint, reached one step differently.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The key engineering was permeability: they designed SIK inhibitors
    specifically to penetrate the human skin barrier. Applied topically, the
    compounds induced pigment in MC1R-deficient mice <em>and</em> darkened intact
    human skin samples, depositing eumelanin near the surface in a pattern
    resembling a UV tan.<Cite n={2} /> This is the strongest evidence to date that a
    true topical tan is achievable in human skin. The honest caveat is equally
    important: these were laboratory and skin-explant studies, not a tested consumer
    product, and a great deal of safety and efficacy work stands between that result
    and anything you could put on your face — deliberately switching on the pigment
    machinery across large areas of real skin is not a step to take lightly.
  </p>

  <h2>Why "just use a melanotan cream" doesn't work</h2>
  <p>
    A reasonable question: if injected melanotan tans people, why not formulate it
    as a lotion and skip the whole barrier problem? Because the barrier <em>is</em>
    the problem. Melanotan and the natural hormone α-MSH are <strong>peptides</strong> —
    relatively large, water-loving molecules that do not cross intact skin in any
    useful amount. That is precisely why grey-market melanotan is injected or
    sprayed into the nose: to get into the bloodstream and reach the receptor from
    the inside.<Cite n={8} /> A peptide rubbed on the skin mostly sits on top of it. The
    small-molecule approaches above — forskolin, and especially the skin-penetrating
    SIK inhibitors — exist because the molecule has to be small and lipophilic
    enough to make the crossing a peptide never will.
  </p>

